1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,776
Your cat can't taste sugar.

2
00:00:01,776 --> 00:00:02,500
Here's why.

3
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:04,514
Cats are obligate carnivores.

4
00:00:04,514 --> 00:00:06,181
Meat is their main fare.

5
00:00:06,181 --> 00:00:10,000
Their sweet-taste equipment
is missing a critical part.

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,075
In 2005, researchers examined
the gene for Tas1r2, one

7
00:00:14,075 --> 00:00:18,000
of two proteins that build
a working sweet receptor.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,161
In cats, that gene has a 247-base-pair
deletion and premature stop signals.

9
00:00:23,161 --> 00:00:25,500
It is a pseudogene, not expressed.

10
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:28,352
The partner gene, Tas1r3,
is still expressed.

11
00:00:28,352 --> 00:00:32,091
But without both halves, the
receptor cannot detect sugars.

12
00:00:32,091 --> 00:00:34,500
Sweetness has no channel to the brain.

13
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,391
The same broken gene appeared in six
domestic cats, a tiger, and a cheetah.

14
00:00:39,391 --> 00:00:40,695
It is not pickiness.

15
00:00:40,695 --> 00:00:42,195
It is deep cat biology.

16
00:00:42,195 --> 00:00:45,000
No sweet tooth; just
a carnivore's toolkit.
